{"The synced response is allowed to omit refresh_token, but the fallback writes the literal string 'refresh_token' into parsed data, which can replace the real refresh token and break later provider refreshes": 0.9495629072189331, "After a Google refresh, the code stores the full parse result as Credential.key instead of the credential fields, making the refreshed credential unusable since the next Google Calendar load expects top-level fields": 0.9618011116981506, "When credential sync is enabled, the helper always returns a native fetch Response, but several updated callers still read provider-specific objects such as tokenInfo.data, hubspotRefreshToken.expiresIn, or res.data, causing refresh paths to throw or persist invalid token fields": 0.959290623664856, "refreshOAuthTokens sends its third argument as calcomUserId, but the call passes credentialId, causing the external app store to receive the credential primary key instead of the Cal.com user id": 0.7987788915634155, "The Salesforce code refreshes tokens and writes the new access token to the database, but then creates the jsforce.Connection from the old credentialKey.access_token, causing immediate calendar operations to run with the stale token": 0.9169993996620178, "The token schema does not require a real expiry field, allowing synced responses with only access_token to pass validation, causing callers to compute expiry from missing fields and potentially store NaN or stale expiry state": 0.7432636022567749, "Salesforce refresh always calls Salesforce directly even when CALCOM_CREDENTIAL_SYNC_ENDPOINT is configured, bypassing the configured source-of-truth endpoint in synced self-hosted setups": 0.9639747738838196, "The webhook decrypts keys and writes the result directly into Credential.key without checking the selected app's expected credential schema, allowing authenticated sync requests to store fields that the app cannot later parse": 0.9345019459724426}